<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.5.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00915</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.5.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>37532d95-0ef6-4e4d-825a-4e0d888f7d2e</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para><emphasis><emphasis>Students will complete the following questions to practice the skills they have learned in this lesson.</emphasis></emphasis></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="1" type="enumerated">
  <item>The total monthly cost of a cell phone plan from Company \(C\) costs $10, plus $15 per gigabyte for data used. The total monthly cost from Company \(D\) costs $80 with unlimited data.</item>
</list>
<para>Rule \(C\) gives the monthly cost, in dollars, of using \(g\) gigabytes of data on Company \(C\)’s plan. Rule \(D\) gives the monthly cost, in dollars, of using \(g\) gigabytes of data on Company \(D\)’s plan. </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="1" type="enumerated">
  <item>Which sentence describes the meaning of the statement \(C(2)=40\)?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>It costs $40 to use 2 gigabytes of data using plan \(C\). </item>
  <item>It costs $40 to use 2 gigabytes of data using plan \(D\).</item>
  <item>It costs $2 to use 40 gigabytes of data using plan \(C\).</item>
  <item>It costs $2 to use 40 gigabytes of data using plan \(D\).</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> It costs $40 to use 2 gigabytes of data using plan \(C\). </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Which is less, \(C(4)\) or \(D(4)\)? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\(C(4)\) is less. \(C(4)=70\) and \(D(4)=80\). The phone plan from Company C is less expensive if 4 gigabytes of data are used.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Which is less, \(C(5)\) or \(D(5)\)?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\(D(5)\) is less. \(D(5)=80\) and \(C(5) =85\). The phone plan from Company D is less expensive if 5 gigabytes of data are used.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>For what number \(g\) is \(C(g)=130\)?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> 8 </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Function \(G\) takes a student’s first name for its input and gives the number of letters in the first name for its output.</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="1" type="enumerated">
  <item>Find the value of of \(G(\text{Jada})=4\). </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>The name “Jada” has 4 letters.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Find the value of \(G(\text{Diego})\). </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>5 </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Is the following graph a function? </item>
</list>

<para><figure><media alt="The figure has an absolute value function graphed on the x y-coordinate plane. The \(x\)-axis runs from negative 6 to 6. The \(y\)-axis runs from negative 4 to 8. The vertex is at the point (0, negative 1). The line goes through the points (negative 1, 0) and (1, 0)."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/8e377a41847030865efbafb8ae4e8c83db43dc88"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>Yes, this is a function because the graph passes the vertical line test.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>Is the following graph a function? </item>
</list>
<para><figure><media alt="The figure has a half-circle graphed on the x y-coordinate plane. The \(x\)-axis runs from negative 6 to 6. The \(y\)-axis runs from negative 6 to 6. The curved line segment starts at the point (negative 2, 0). The line goes through the point (0, 2) and ends at the point (2, 0)."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/1f2d86b6714ef87d94be2f3aa4741273c2f2f4d5"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>Yes, this is a function because the graph passes the vertical line test.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>Is the following graph a function? </item>
</list>
<para><figure><media alt="The figure has two curved lines graphed on the x y-coordinate plane. The \(x\)-axis runs from negative 6 to 6. The \(y\)-axis runs from negative 6 to 6. The curved line on the left goes through the points (negative 2, 0), (negative 4, 5), and (negative 4, negative 5). The curved line on the right goes through the points (2, 0), (4, 5), and (4, negative 5)."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b27425bc88681f1b290617df73d1d63458f6116c"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>No, this is not a function because the graph does not pass the vertical line test. </para></section>
